  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nWe propose a Bayesian optimization combined with random noise insertion. Instead of using gradient descent or evolutionary methods, we maintain a simplex-normalized sequence and systematically add small random perturbations to its successor's supports with appropriate scaling. This approach leverages the relative importance of different function locations as suggested by the Srivastava example, but we simplify by only tracking a single, fixed-length sequence and comparing only the necessary peaks during evaluation.\nThe central idea is to construct a sequence that directly minimizes the objective by focusing on the normalized convolution peaks. We do this by maintaining a fixed-length candidate (N=448, as the previous worked copy) and iteratively updating it using Bayesian optimization to balance exploration and exploitation, while relying on exhaustive evaluation to confirm fitness and guide changes.\n\nMathematical Specification\nWe use a fixed-length sequence (N=448). Cuisenaire we store only one candidate sequence at a time, which we maintain in a simplex-normalised form (sum=1). The objective is simply 2*N/max(b_sequence)/sum(sequence)\u00b2, but after our normalization sum is always 1 so that f part becomes 2*N/max(b_sequence).\nThe constraints are: each element non-negative, and the normalized sum must be computed correctly we can't override anything, only rely on the evaluator's processing for each candidate. \n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Start with a uniform random solution (dl=448, each term random but positive, sum=1).\n2. For each iteration:\n   - Evaluate the current solution (using evaluate_sequence)\n   - Generate the full-length auto-convolution b_sequence (length 2N-1)\n   - Keep track of the best score (lowest objective value so far)\n   - Use Bayesian optimization to choose next update:\n     \u25cb Throughput expected to be less than 1800 seconds; take around 1000 steps (step count will be much less than 1800 due to evaluation cost).\n     \u25cb For each step, sample random weights from the normalized distribution of important points (i.e., the coefficients that achieved higher convolution values) and update the candidate accordingly.\n3. Maintain a set of important positions in the first convolution, which we obtain simply by sorting the convolution values and retaining the top 10% (or all, but bounded).\n4. Update the candidate by perturbing the coefficients at these important positions, scaling by a small random factor in (-0.1, 0.1). Non-negativity must always be preserved.\n5. For each candidate evaluation, we return the exact sequence without any rescaling beyond simplex projection (normalize by sum, but sum already 1).\n6. We store the candidate that achieves the best score so far, and if we achieve a score < 2*N/max(b_sequence)/sum\u00b2 but in the normalized form the denominator is sum\u00b2 (we keep sum=1). After exceeding time, return the best tracker candidate.\n\nSearch Choices and Useful Variations\n- N is fixed at 448 (closest working copy length). Varying length would not be pursued.\n- Bayesian parameters: learning rate \u03b1=0.5, exploration factor \u03b2=0.9 (this can be tuned).\n- Important positions: sorted convolution and take top 40 (or more but bounded by performance).\n\nCompute Budget and Robustness\nUp to 1800 seconds; we expect around 1000 iterations. If no improvement happens early, we skip to a more informed perturbation.\n\nValidation and Return Contract\nAt the end, output the best-runtime sequence (by sum of important positions or the highest convolution score) trimmed with evaluate_sequence. If we don't find any improvement, fallback to the initial uniform random at N=448.",
